(455236, 40708, 5728, '<td valign="top">Thank you for all your comments on this pic, I had a lot of fun doing it. Pia is only 15 years old! You wouldn''t think it looking at this pic.<br/> <br/>@ BobsterLobster: the white balance is set appropriately for the studio lighting set up I had. And as for "underexposed", I am not sure what you mean there, the levels graph showed I had the correct exposure.<br/> <br/>@ dsa157: I think sadness is not something a bride would be expressing in a situation like this. I would expect a mixture of emptyness, anger and confusion. I think the sadness stage would come later.<br/> <br/>@ Fayepek: That would have been a good title too, this was the best I could come up with :-) Too many highlights on her face? Funny, SharQ said it was underexposed.<br/> <br/>@ Jon Lucas and dsidwell: your comments contradict each other :-)<br/> <br/>@ Olyuzi: thank you for that comment, your first sentence sums it all up well.<br/> <br/>Thanks again everyone for your comments.<br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'Thank you for all your comments on this pic, I had a lot of fun doing it. Pia is only 15 years old! You wouldn''t think it looking at this pic. @ BobsterLobster: the white balance is set appropriately for the studio lighting set up I had. And as for "underexposed", I am not sure what you mean there, the levels graph showed I had the correct exposure. @ dsa157: I think sadness is not something a bride would be expressing in a situation like this. I would expect a mixture of emptyness, anger and confusion. I think the sadness stage would come later. @ Fayepek: That would have been a good title too, this was the best I could come up with :-) Too many highlights on her face? Funny, SharQ said it was underexposed. @ Jon Lucas and dsidwell: your comments contradict each other :-) @ Olyuzi: thank you for that comment, your first sentence sums it all up well. Thanks again everyone for your comments.  ', '2003-10-19 18:02:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(455258, 40833, 280, '<td valign="top">Hey Robin,<br/> <br/>Unfortunalty I didnt vote on the Dreams and Nightmares challenge, however I do really like this shot. <br/>The idea of a falling dream/nightmare works well as I guess it relates to a lot of people, I know it does me. That along with the zoom effect, an effect I have seen used a few times on DPC before and it really hasnt worked for me. In the past I dont think the feeling of motion generally suits the pictures I have seen. However in this case the effect creates the main impact of the image. A kind of interaction between the photograph and the viewer, almost like a magic eye.<br/> <br/>As far as the actual scene its self is concerned it feels as if it is cropped a little too much, I think I would have preferred a wider angle to show more of the scene. Although I guess that would have made it harder to get the required angle to give the impression of falling, without taking the photograph from a higher position. Although I guess the best tribute to this photo is the number of people who actually experienced the falling sensation :-)<br/> <br/>I dont know if it was done for a reason but personally, I would like to have the horizontal and vertical borders the same width. But overall very well done. <br/> <br/>Tom.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hey Robin, Unfortunalty I didnt vote on the Dreams and Nightmares challenge, however I do really like this shot. The idea of a falling dream/nightmare works well as I guess it relates to a lot of people, I know it does me. That along with the zoom effect, an effect I have seen used a few times on DPC before and it really hasnt worked for me. In the past I dont think the feeling of motion generally suits the pictures I have seen. However in this case the effect creates the main impact of the image. A kind of interaction between the photograph and the viewer, almost like a magic eye. As far as the actual scene its self is concerned it feels as if it is cropped a little too much, I think I would have preferred a wider angle to show more of the scene. Although I guess that would have made it harder to get the required angle to give the impression of falling, without taking the photograph from a higher position. Although I guess the best tribute to this photo is the number of people who actually experienced the falling sensation :-) I dont know if it was done for a reason but personally, I would like to have the horizontal and vertical borders the same width. But overall very well done.  Tom. ', '2003-10-19 19:05:37.000000', NULL, 0),
